{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017tB1xdq9755SYQ179EbQXQ","type":"tool_result","content":"2:## *\"Flat networks, stable names, and the abstraction that survives churn\"*\n9:## Attention Budget\n43:## 🧭 Soundings\n92:## Why This Chapter Matters\n110:## What You'll Learn\n129:## ⚪ §1 — Four Rules and a Plugin\n180:★ **Fixed Point:** Every Pod gets a unique **cluster-wide** IP address, and **all Pods can reach all Pods without NAT and without proxies** — same node or different nodes. The Pod holds the address; its containers share it and reach each other over `localhost`.\n182:### Kubernetes implements none of this\n194:★ **Fixed Point:** Kubernetes **defines** the network model. A **CNI network plugin implements** it. The four rules above are requirements the plugin satisfies, not machinery Kubernetes provides [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23].\n208:## ⚪ §2 — The Address That Doesn't Last\n230:★ **Fixed Point:** A Service is a stable, long-lived address for a set of Pods **that is expected to change**. It is not a workaround for churn; it is the abstraction that makes churn a non-event.\n244:## 🔵 §3 — Four Ways to Be Reachable\n248:### The three that stack\n287:★ **Fixed Point:** The three ladder types are **additive**. A NodePort Service also has a cluster IP. A LoadBalancer Service sits on top of both. Asking for a higher rung never removes the rungs below it.\n289:### The one that is not on the ladder\n299:### The other fact that catches people\n309:★ **Fixed Point:** **Kubernetes provides no load balancer.** `type: LoadBalancer` is a request for one from somewhere else — your cloud provider, or a component you install.\n313:### Choosing\n326:### The ceiling\n338:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1\n356:**1. The sending Pod's own IP address. Not involved: NAT (address translation), and proxies.**\n364:**2. No — they share one port space. Over `localhost`. One address.**\n372:**3. Nothing was lost. Requesting NodePort also allocates a cluster IP.**\n378:**4. Every Pod is replaced, and each replacement has a different address. A Service gives the client one address that does not change while the set behind it does.**\n384:**5. LoadBalancer: waiting for an external load balancer that nothing is providing. ExternalName: working exactly as designed, as a CNAME with no proxying. Neither is broken.**\n409:## 🔵 §4 — The List Behind the Name\n413:### The path, in four steps\n453:### Selection is not ownership\n459:### Readiness gates membership\n469:★ **Fixed Point:** **selector → EndpointSlice → traffic.** The selector proposes; readiness disposes. A Pod must both **match the selector** and be **Ready** to appear in the Service's EndpointSlice and receive traffic.\n473:### The same fact, running backwards\n485:### Looking at the list\n503:### The case that closes the section\n513:## 🟡 §5 — When You Don't Want a Single Address\n517:### Headless: no single address, on purpose\n526:★ **Fixed Point:** `clusterIP: None` is a **configuration, not a failure**. It says: *do not give me one address — give me all of them.* DNS returns the Pod addresses directly instead of a single virtual IP.\n530:### Why anyone would want that\n540:### Services without selectors\n550:### Two binaries, four cases\n563:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2\n583:**1. The EndpointSlice. Written by the EndpointSlice controller.**\n591:**2. Three endpoints. When the fourth Pod's probe starts passing, it is added to the EndpointSlice and begins receiving traffic.**\n597:**3. Either the Service's selector doesn't match the Pods' labels, or the Pods are not Ready. Tell them apart by comparing the Service's selector against the Pods' labels, and by checking the Pods' Ready condition.**\n608:**4. It means: don't allocate one virtual IP for this Service. DNS returns the Pod addresses directly instead. Correct choice for a StatefulSet.**\n614:**5. A Service without a selector, backed by manually managed EndpointSlices; or `type: ExternalName`. The difference is proxying: the first puts kube-proxy in the path, the second does not.**\n638:## 🔵 §6 — The Component That Makes It Real\n642:### One component, one job\n654:### The cluster IP is virtual\n694:★ **Fixed Point:** kube-proxy implements the virtual IP mechanism for **every Service type except ExternalName**, by watching **Service and EndpointSlice** objects and programming each node. Modes: **iptables (the default)**, IPVS, nftables on Linux; **kernelspace** on Windows.\n696:### The modes\n709:### kube-proxy is optional\n723:## 🔵 §7 — Names, and Where They Resolve\n729:### What serves the records\n739:### The Service record\n754:### The same name, a different answer\n764:★ **Fixed Point:** The **same name form** — `<service>.<namespace>.svc.<cluster-domain>` — gives you the **cluster IP** for a normal Service and **the set of Pod IPs** for a headless one.\n766:### Why a bare name works, and where it stops\n776:★ **Fixed Point:** `<service>.<namespace>.svc.<cluster-domain>`. A **bare name resolves in the client Pod's own namespace only**, because that namespace is in its DNS search list. Crossing a namespace boundary means using the full name.\n782:### The other record shapes\n838:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3\n856:**1. kube-proxy. It does not implement ExternalName. It watches Service and EndpointSlice objects.**\n862:**2. Nothing. The cluster IP is virtual — no process is bound to it.**\n872:**3. iptables (the default), IPVS, and nftables on Linux; kernelspace on Windows. A cluster with no kube-proxy means the network plugin implements Service packet forwarding itself.**\n878:**4. `ledger.billing.svc.cluster.local`. A bare `ledger` would resolve within `payments` only — failing, or worse, silently reaching a different `ledger`.**\n886:**5. `db-a` returns one address — the Service's cluster IP. `db-b` returns the set of IPs of all the Pods it selects.**\n905:## ⚪ §8 — A Query With a Name\n950:☀️ **Zenith:** A Service is a **label query with a name**. The virtual IP, the endpoint list, and the DNS record are three control loops publishing that one query's current answer in three different formats. The Pods were never the stable thing. **The question was.**\n956:### Where the claim overreaches\n968:### The boundary\n978:## Exam Alert! 🚨\n1020:## Practice Questions\n1026:**1.** Which of the following is required by the Kubernetes network model?\n1035:**2.** A Pod is running three containers. **[retrieval: ch5]** How many IP addresses does the cluster allocate for that Pod, and how many entries would that Pod contribute to a matching Service's EndpointSlice?\n1044:**3.** Which statement about CNI is correct?\n1053:**4.** Why does a Pod's IP address make a poor thing for a client to store in its configuration?\n1062:**5.** A Service is created with no `type` field specified. What type is it, and from where is it reachable?\n1071:**6.** You change a Service from `type: ClusterIP` to `type: NodePort`. What happens to the cluster IP?\n1080:**7.** Your team runs Kubernetes on bare metal with no cloud-provider integration and no load-balancer add-on installed. You create a `type: LoadBalancer` Service. What happens?\n1089:**8.** Which Service type sets up **no proxying of any kind**?\n1098:**9.** You need clients inside the cluster to reach `analytics.vendor.example`, a SaaS endpoint outside the cluster, using an in-cluster name. Which Service configuration produces a CNAME with Kubernetes entirely out of the traffic path?\n1107:**10.** **[retrieval: ch4]** A Pod carries labels that a ReplicaSet selects on and labels that a Service selects on. Someone edits one of those labels. Name both consequences, and say whether the two controllers coordinate.\n1116:**11.** Where does Kubernetes record the set of Pods currently backing a Service, and what writes it?\n1125:**12.** **[retrieval: ch5]** Chapter 5 said a readiness probe controls whether a Pod receives traffic. Name the object that fact is actually implemented in, and say what a failing probe changes about it.\n1134:**13.** A Service's selector matches six Pods. Four are Ready, one is failing its readiness probe, and one is terminating gracefully. What does the Service's EndpointSlice contain?\n1143:**14.** `kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web` returns a slice with no endpoints. The Pods you expect are Running. What are the two usual causes?\n1152:**15.** A colleague reports: \"I created the Service, it has a cluster IP and a DNS record, but nothing responds and there are zero endpoints.\" What is the correct characterisation?\n1161:**16.** What does setting `.spec.clusterIP` to `None` do, and which workload type requires it?\n1170:**17.** You are migrating a database into the cluster. Today it runs on external hardware; next month it will run as Pods. You want in-cluster clients to use one unchanging Service name throughout, with traffic proxied by the cluster in both phases. What do you configure today?\n1179:**18.** kube-proxy's modes on Linux are:\n1188:**19.** An engineer SSHes to a worker node and runs `ss -tlnp` looking for a process bound to a Service's cluster IP. What will they find, and why?\n1197:**20.** Your cluster is running normally, serving traffic to Services, and there are no kube-proxy Pods anywhere. What is happening, and which of §1's four rules is the same component also satisfying?\n1206:**21.** **[retrieval: ch4]** Chapter 4 told you a bare service name resolves within the local namespace. What makes that true — what is configured, and by which component?\n1219:**1. B.**\n1225:**2. D. One address, one entry.**\n1231:**3. B.**\n1237:**4. C.**\n1243:**5. B.**\n1249:**6. B.**\n1255:**7. C.**\n1263:**8. D.**\n1269:**9. B.**\n1275:**10. C.**\n1281:**11. B.**\n1287:**12. C.**\n1293:**13. C.**\n1299:**14. B.**\n1305:**15. B.**\n1313:**16. C.**\n1319:**17. C.**\n1327:**18. B.**\n1333:**19. C.**\n1339:**20. B.**\n1345:**21. B.**\n1355:## Chapter Summary\n1389:## The Voyage Ahead"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d474c952-875b-43f6-8fc1-a2a7f27f8ec4","uuid":"58732588-8b1d-4443-8837-63c151dddee7","timestamp":"2026-08-25T01:23:24.949Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"2:## *\"Flat networks, stable names, and the abstraction that survives churn\"*\n9:## Attention Budget\n43:## 🧭 Soundings\n92:## Why This Chapter Matters\n110:## What You'll Learn\n129:## ⚪ §1 — Four Rules and a Plugin\n180:★ **Fixed Point:** Every Pod gets a unique **cluster-wide** IP address, and **all Pods can reach all Pods without NAT and without proxies** — same node or different nodes. The Pod holds the address; its containers share it and reach each other over `localhost`.\n182:### Kubernetes implements none of this\n194:★ **Fixed Point:** Kubernetes **defines** the network model. A **CNI network plugin implements** it. The four rules above are requirements the plugin satisfies, not machinery Kubernetes provides [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23].\n208:## ⚪ §2 — The Address That Doesn't Last\n230:★ **Fixed Point:** A Service is a stable, long-lived address for a set of Pods **that is expected to change**. It is not a workaround for churn; it is the abstraction that makes churn a non-event.\n244:## 🔵 §3 — Four Ways to Be Reachable\n248:### The three that stack\n287:★ **Fixed Point:** The three ladder types are **additive**. A NodePort Service also has a cluster IP. A LoadBalancer Service sits on top of both. Asking for a higher rung never removes the rungs below it.\n289:### The one that is not on the ladder\n299:### The other fact that catches people\n309:★ **Fixed Point:** **Kubernetes provides no load balancer.** `type: LoadBalancer` is a request for one from somewhere else — your cloud provider, or a component you install.\n313:### Choosing\n326:### The ceiling\n338:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1\n356:**1. The sending Pod's own IP address. Not involved: NAT (address translation), and proxies.**\n364:**2. 