{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ASoMkvAkdnsG77k2s1D7MN","type":"tool_result","content":"338\t## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1\n339\t\n340\tFive questions on the model and the abstraction — §1 through §3. Two of them reach back to earlier chapters.\n341\t\n342\t1. ⚪ A Pod on `worker-3` sends a request to a Pod on `worker-11`. What does the receiving Pod see as the source address, and what two things does the network model guarantee are *not* involved?\n343\t\n344\t2. ⚪ **[retrieval: ch5]** A Pod runs two containers. One listens on 8080. Can the other also listen on 8080? How does each reach the other? And how many IP addresses does the Pod have?\n345\t\n346\t3. 🔵 You create a Service with `type: NodePort`. A colleague says, \"so now it's only reachable from outside — we lost the internal address.\" What is wrong with that, and why?\n347\t\n348\t4. 🔵 **[retrieval: ch6]** Chapter 6 walked a Deployment through a rolling update. State what happens to the Pod IP addresses during that update, and then say what a Service gives a client that the Pod IPs cannot.\n349\t\n350\t5. 🔵 Two Services. One has `type: LoadBalancer` and, twenty minutes after creation, still has no external address. One has `type: ExternalName` pointing at `api.vendor.example`. For each: what is Kubernetes doing, and is anything broken?\n351\t\n352\t---\n353\t\n354\t**Answers with Explanations:**\n355\t\n356\t**1. The sending Pod's own IP address. Not involved: NAT (address translation), and proxies.**\n357\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d474c952-875b-43f6-8fc1-a2a7f27f8ec4","uuid":"3d529b3f-a22a-41a0-932b-fce0da2094ab","timestamp":"2026-08-25T01:23:29.783Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-09/draft-v1.md","content":"## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1\n\nFive questions on the model and the abstraction — §1 through §3. Two of them reach back to earlier chapters.\n\n1. ⚪ A Pod on `worker-3` sends a request to a Pod on `worker-11`. What does the receiving Pod see as the source address, and what two things does the network model guarantee are *not* involved?\n\n2. ⚪ **[retrieval: ch5]** A Pod runs two containers. One listens on 8080. Can the other also listen on 8080? How does each reach the other? And how many IP addresses does the Pod have?\n\n3. 🔵 You create a Service with `type: NodePort`. A colleague says, \"so now it's only reachable from outside — we lost the internal address.\" What is wrong with that, and why?\n\n4. 🔵 **[retrieval: ch6]** Chapter 6 walked a Deployment through a rolling update. State what happens to the Pod IP addresses during that update, and then say what a Service gives a client that the Pod IPs cannot.\n\n5. 🔵 Two Services. One has `type: LoadBalancer` and, twenty minutes after creation, still has no external address. One has `type: ExternalName` pointing at `api.vendor.example`. For each: what is Kubernetes doing, and is anything broken?\n\n---\n\n**Answers with Explanations:**\n\n**1. The sending Pod's own IP address. Not involved: NAT (address translation), and proxies.**\n","numLines":20,"startLine":338,"totalLines":1402}}}
