{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Scj9PmCTQVWRa8Cx7zsg3b","type":"tool_result","content":"563\t## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2\n564\t\n565\tFive questions on what's actually behind the name, and the two times you deliberately don't want one — §4 and §5.\n566\t\n567\t1. 🔵 **[retrieval: ch6]** Chapter 6 said a ReplicaSet finds its Pods by selector, and that a Service asking the same question about the same Pods is a *different controller reading the same labels*. Name the object where the Service's answer gets written down, and name the controller that writes it.\n568\t\n569\t2. 🔵 A Service's selector matches four Pods. Three are Ready; one is failing its readiness probe. How many endpoints does the Service have, and what happens to the fourth Pod when its probe starts passing?\n570\t\n571\t3. 🟡 `kubectl get endpointslices` for your Service returns no endpoints. The Pods are running. Name the two usual causes, and say how you would tell them apart.\n572\t\n573\t4. 🔵 A teammate calls a headless Service \"broken\" because `kubectl` shows no cluster IP. Explain what `clusterIP: None` does, and give one workload for which it is the correct choice.\n574\t\n575\t5. 🔵 You need cluster-internal clients to reach a database that runs on hardware outside the cluster, using an ordinary Service name. Two Service configurations could do this. Name both, and say what is different about what the client experiences.\n576\t\n577\t> ⚠️ **Question 3 is intentionally challenging.** Your instinct will be to look at the network. The answer is in two YAML files. If you struggle with it, that struggle is the point — this is the item most likely to save you real time later, and it encodes better for having been hard.\n578\t\n579\t---\n580\t\n581\t**Answers with Explanations:**\n582\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d474c952-875b-43f6-8fc1-a2a7f27f8ec4","uuid":"bcbb0ed2-6b30-4600-808f-3569d79bc391","timestamp":"2026-08-25T01:23:36.996Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-09/draft-v1.md","content":"## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2\n\nFive questions on what's actually behind the name, and the two times you deliberately don't want one — §4 and §5.\n\n1. 🔵 **[retrieval: ch6]** Chapter 6 said a ReplicaSet finds its Pods by selector, and that a Service asking the same question about the same Pods is a *different controller reading the same labels*. Name the object where the Service's answer gets written down, and name the controller that writes it.\n\n2. 🔵 A Service's selector matches four Pods. Three are Ready; one is failing its readiness probe. How many endpoints does the Service have, and what happens to the fourth Pod when its probe starts passing?\n\n3. 🟡 `kubectl get endpointslices` for your Service returns no endpoints. The Pods are running. Name the two usual causes, and say how you would tell them apart.\n\n4. 🔵 A teammate calls a headless Service \"broken\" because `kubectl` shows no cluster IP. Explain what `clusterIP: None` does, and give one workload for which it is the correct choice.\n\n5. 🔵 You need cluster-internal clients to reach a database that runs on hardware outside the cluster, using an ordinary Service name. Two Service configurations could do this. Name both, and say what is different about what the client experiences.\n\n> ⚠️ **Question 3 is intentionally challenging.** Your instinct will be to look at the network. The answer is in two YAML files. If you struggle with it, that struggle is the point — this is the item most likely to save you real time later, and it encodes better for having been hard.\n\n---\n\n**Answers with Explanations:**\n","numLines":20,"startLine":563,"totalLines":1402}}}
