{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JkbijoTDviJhubpPvtVJdf","type":"tool_result","content":"200:One practical consequence, and it retrieves last chapter at exactly one chapter's distance: a node whose network is not correctly configured reports the **`NetworkUnavailable`** condition — `True` if the network for the node is not correctly configured [source: k8s-docs-nodes-2026-08-23]. You met the node condition list in Chapter 8 *[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 §4 — node conditions]*. This is the shortest demonstration available that the plugin is not a footnote.\n733:The server doing the answering is **CoreDNS** — the cluster DNS addon that serves these records [source: k8s-docs-dns-pod-service-2026-08-23], listed under Service Discovery in the cluster addons as a flexible, extensible DNS server which can be installed as the in-cluster DNS for Pods [source: k8s-docs-cluster-addons-2026-08-24].\n735:And the reason you have never installed it, never configured it, and possibly never thought about it: **DNS is a built-in Kubernetes service launched automatically using the addon manager cluster add-on** [source: k8s-docs-dns-cluster-addon-2026-08-24].\n737:Chapter 3 promised you CoreDNS by name *[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §6 — cluster addons and CoreDNS]*, and Chapter 4 gave you a Service's DNS name in a single sentence and explicitly deferred four things: the mechanism, what serves the records, what else gets one, and how resolution actually proceeds *[cross-bearing: see Ch 4 §6 — namespaces and Service DNS names]*. All four are discharged in this section.\n786:**SRV records** are created for **named ports** that are part of normal or headless Services, with the form `_port-name._port-protocol.my-svc.my-namespace.svc.cluster-domain.example` [source: k8s-docs-dns-pod-service-2026-08-23]. Note that the Service's own four labels are intact at the end; the port-name and protocol are prefixed on.\n788:**Pods** get records too. In general a Pod has the DNS resolution of the form `pod-ipv4-address.my-namespace.pod.cluster-domain.example` — for example, `172-17-0-3.default.pod.cluster.local` [source: k8s-docs-dns-pod-service-2026-08-23]. The dots in the address become dashes, because dots are label separators in DNS and cannot appear inside a label. Note also that position 3 is `pod`, not `svc`.\n790:And the one that finishes the StatefulSet story: the Pod spec has optional **`hostname` and `subdomain`** fields; **when both are set and a headless Service exists with the same name as the subdomain, the Pod's FQDN is `hostname.subdomain.namespace.svc.cluster-domain.example`** [source: k8s-docs-dns-pod-service-2026-08-23].\n803:  SRV (named port)  │ _http._tcp.   │ my-namespace │  svc  │ cluster.local   │  → the named port\n806:  Pod (by address)  │  172-17-0-3   │ my-namespace │  pod  │ cluster.local   │  → that Pod\n810:     subdomain)     │   subdomain   │              │       │                 │     stable name\n825:> | `pod-ipv4-address.my-namespace.pod.cluster-domain.example` (e.g. `172-17-0-3.default.pod.cluster.local`) | That Pod |\n826:> | `hostname.subdomain.namespace.svc.cluster-domain.example` (requires `hostname` + `subdomain` set, and a headless Service named for the subdomain) | That Pod, by a stable name |\n828:> Record types are A and/or AAAA depending on the IP family, except SRV records, which are SRV. Cluster DNS is served by CoreDNS, launched automatically by the addon manager. Pod DNS configuration is written by the kubelet. A bare service name resolves within the client Pod's own namespace because that namespace is in the Pod's default DNS search list. [source: k8s-docs-dns-pod-service-2026-08-23] [source: k8s-docs-dns-cluster-addon-2026-08-24]\n834:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: outline explicitly scopes out CoreDNS Corefile configuration, custom nameservers, stub domains, and CoreDNS plugins. The dns-cluster-addon snapshot's own note records the rest of that page as out of scope. Nothing on cluster DNS customisation appears above; that omission is deliberate, not a gap. -->\n1121:D) In cluster DNS, written by CoreDNS\n1193:D) CoreDNS, because it owns all cluster-internal addresses\n1208:A) CoreDNS rewrites bare queries to the caller's namespace, using the caller's source IP\n1337:**A** and **B** both assume something terminates the connection at the cluster IP. **D** confuses DNS with addressing entirely — CoreDNS answers name queries; it owns no addresses."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d474c952-875b-43f6-8fc1-a2a7f27f8ec4","uuid":"07d27e26-2160-4240-8e3c-f3f9a080d691","timestamp":"2026-08-25T01:24:24.777Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"200:One practical consequence, and it retrieves last chapter at exactly one chapter's distance: a node whose network is not correctly configured reports the **`NetworkUnavailable`** condition — `True` if the network for the node is not correctly configured [source: k8s-docs-nodes-2026-08-23]. You met the node condition list in Chapter 8 *[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 §4 — node conditions]*. This is the shortest demonstration available that the plugin is not a footnote.\n733:The server doing the answering is **CoreDNS** — the cluster DNS addon that serves these records [source: k8s-docs-dns-pod-service-2026-08-23], listed under Service Discovery in the cluster addons as a flexible, extensible DNS server which can be installed as the in-cluster DNS for Pods [source: k8s-docs-cluster-addons-2026-08-24].\n735:And the reason you have never installed it, never configured it, and possibly never thought about it: **DNS is a built-in Kubernetes service launched automatically using the addon manager cluster add-on** [source: k8s-docs-dns-cluster-addon-2026-08-24].\n737:Chapter 3 promised you CoreDNS by name *[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §6 — cluster addons and CoreDNS]*, and Chapter 4 gave you a Service's DNS name in a single sentence and explicitly deferred four things: the mechanism, what serves the records, what else gets one, and how resolution actually proceeds *[cross-bearing: see Ch 4 §6 — namespaces and Service DNS names]*. All four are discharged in this section.\n786:**SRV records** are created for **named ports** that are part of normal or headless Services, with the form `_port-name._port-protocol.my-svc.my-namespace.svc.cluster-domain.example` [source: k8s-docs-dns-pod-service-2026-08-23]. Note that the Service's own four labels are intact at the end; the port-name and protocol are prefixed on.\n788:**Pods** get records too. In general a Pod has the DNS resolution of the form `pod-ipv4-address.my-namespace.pod.cluster-domain.example` — for example, `172-17-0-3.default.pod.cluster.local` [source: k8s-docs-dns-pod-service-2026-08-23]. The dots in the address become dashes, because dots are label separators in DNS and cannot appear inside a label. Note also that position 3 is `pod`, not `svc`.\n790:And the one that finishes the StatefulSet story: the Pod spec has optional **`hostname` and `subdomain`** fields; **when both are set and a headless Service exists with the same name as the subdomain, the Pod's FQDN is `hostname.subdomain.namespace.svc.cluster-domain.example`** [source: k8s-docs-dns-pod-service-2026-08-23].\n803:  SRV (named port)  │ _http._tcp.   │ my-namespace │  svc  │ cluster.local   │  → the named port\n806:  Pod (by address)  │  172-17-0-3   │ my-namespace │  pod  │ cluster.local   │  → that Pod\n810:     subdomain)     │   subdomain   │              │       │                 │     stable name\n825:> | `pod-ipv4-address.my-namespace.pod.cluster-domain.example` (e.g. `172-17-0-3.default.pod.cluster.local`) | That Pod |\n826:> | `hostname.subdomain.namespace.svc.cluster-domain.example` (requires `hostname` + `subdomain` set, and a headless Service named for the subdomain) | That Pod, by a stable name |\n828:> Record types are A and/or AAAA depending on the IP family, except SRV records, which are SRV. Cluster DNS is served by CoreDNS, launched automatically by the addon manager. Pod DNS configuration is written by the kubelet. A bare service name resolves within the client Pod's own namespace because that namespace is in the Pod's default DNS search list. [source: k8s-docs-dns-pod-service-2026-08-23] [source: k8s-docs-dns-cluster-addon-2026-08-24]\n834:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: outline explicitly scopes out CoreDNS Corefile configuration, custom nameservers, stub domains, and CoreDNS plugins. The dns-cluster-addon snapshot's own note records the rest of that page as out of scope. Nothing on cluster DNS customisation appears above; that omission is deliberate, not a gap. -->\n1121:D) In cluster DNS, written by CoreDNS\n1193:D) CoreDNS, because it owns all cluster-internal addresses\n1208:A) CoreDNS rewrites bare queries to the caller's namespace, using the caller's source IP\n1337:**A** and **B** both assume something terminates the connection at the cluster IP. **D** confuses DNS with addressing entirely — CoreDNS answers name queries; it owns no addresses.","numLines":18,"totalLines":18}}
